1. What is Data Communication?
Definition

Data Communication is the exchange of data between two or more devices through a transmission medium.

In simple words:

When one device sends information and another device receives it, data communication happens.

Example 1: Sending a WhatsApp Message

You send:

Hello

Behind the scenes:

Your Phone
     |
     |
 Converts message into bits
     |
     |
 Network
     |
     |
 WhatsApp Server
     |
     |
 Friend's Phone

The message is converted into digital signals and transmitted through the network.

Example 2: Opening a Website

You type:

www.google.com

Communication happens:

Your Computer

      ↓

Router

      ↓

ISP

      ↓

Internet

      ↓

Google Server

      ↓

Web Page Response

This entire exchange is data communication.

2. Components of Data Communication

For successful communication, five components are required.

1. Sender

The device that sends data.

Examples:

Laptop
Mobile phone
Server

Example:

You sending an email.

Sender = Your laptop

2. Receiver

The device that receives data.

Examples:

Another computer
Mobile phone
Server

Example:

Receiver = Friend's email server

3. Message

The actual information being transmitted.

Examples:

Text
Image
Video
Audio
Files

Example:

"Meeting at 5 PM"

is the message.

4. Transmission Medium

The path through which data travels.

Examples:

Wired:

Ethernet cable
Fiber optic cable

Wireless:

Wi-Fi
Bluetooth
Satellite
5. Protocol

The rules that control communication.

Examples:

HTTP
TCP
IP
DNS
SMTP
Real-Life Analogy: Phone Call

Imagine calling your friend.

Communication Component	Example
Sender	You
Receiver	Friend
Message	Voice
Medium	Mobile network
Protocol	Rules of telephone communication

Without rules, communication would fail.

3. Characteristics of Effective Data Communication

A good communication system should have:

1. Delivery

Data must reach the correct destination.

Example:

You send an email to:

abc@gmail.com

It should not reach:

xyz@gmail.com
2. Accuracy

Data should arrive without errors.

Example:

Original:

Password123

Received:

Passw0rd123

This is inaccurate.

3. Timeliness

Data should arrive on time.

Important for:

Video calls
Online gaming
Stock trading

Example:

A 5-second delay in a live video call affects communication.

4. Jitter

Jitter means variation in delay between packets.

Example:

Suppose you are in a video call.

Packets arrive like:

Packet 1 → 10 ms

Packet 2 → 20 ms

Packet 3 → 200 ms

The difference causes:

Voice breaks
Video freezing

This is jitter.

4. Data Representation

Computers cannot understand human language directly.

They understand:

Binary

which consists of:

0 and 1
Example

Human language:

Hello

Computer representation:

01001000 01100101 01101100 01101100 01101111

Everything is converted into bits.

5. Data Flow Direction

Communication between devices can happen in three ways.

A. Simplex Communication
Definition

Data travels in only one direction.

Sender  -------------> Receiver

The receiver cannot send data back.

Examples
Television Broadcast
TV Station

      ↓

Your TV

You receive signals but cannot send anything back.

Keyboard

Traditional keyboard:

Keyboard

     ↓

Computer

Data only goes from keyboard to computer.

Advantages
Simple
Cheap
Disadvantages
No two-way communication
B. Half Duplex Communication
Definition

Data can travel in both directions, but only one direction at a time.

Device A  --------> Device B

Device A  <-------- Device B

Both can communicate, but not simultaneously.

Example
Walkie-Talkie

Person A:

"Hello"

(wait)

Person B:

"Hello"

Only one person speaks at a time.

Advantages
Two-way communication
Less complex
Disadvantages
Slower than full duplex
C. Full Duplex Communication
Definition

Data can travel in both directions at the same time.

Device A  <============> Device B
Examples
Phone Call

You can:

Speak
Listen

at the same time.

Modern Ethernet Networks

Most switched networks support full duplex communication.

Comparison
Type	Direction	Example
Simplex	One way	TV broadcast
Half Duplex	Both ways, one at a time	Walkie-talkie
Full Duplex	Both ways simultaneously	Phone call
6. Transmission Modes

Another way to classify communication is:

Unicast

One sender → One receiver

Example:

Sending an email to one person.

A --------> B
Broadcast

One sender → Everyone on the network

Example:

A hub sending data.

       B
      /
A -------- C
      \
       D
Multicast

One sender → Selected group

Example:

Live video streaming to subscribed users.

        B
       /
A ---- C
       \
        D
7. Bandwidth
Definition

Bandwidth is the maximum amount of data that can be transmitted through a network in a given time.

Measured in:

bps (bits per second)
Mbps
Gbps
Example

Internet connection:

100 Mbps

means theoretically:

100 million bits can be transmitted per second.

Analogy

Think about a highway.

Bandwidth = Number of lanes

More lanes:

More vehicles
More traffic capacity
8. Throughput
Definition

Throughput is the actual amount of data successfully transferred.

Bandwidth is the capability.

Throughput is the reality.

Example:

Internet plan:

Bandwidth: 100 Mbps

Actual speed:

Throughput: 80 Mbps

because of:

Network congestion
Distance
Hardware limitations
9. Latency
Definition

Latency is the time taken for data to travel from sender to receiver.

Measured in:

Milliseconds (ms)

Example:

Online gaming:

Low latency:

20 ms

Good experience.

High latency:

300 ms

Lag occurs.

10. Packets

Data is not sent as one huge piece.

It is divided into smaller units called:

Packets

Example:

You send a large video file.

Instead of:

VIDEO FILE

Network sends:

Packet 1
Packet 2
Packet 3
Packet 4
...

Each packet contains:

Source address
Destination address
Data
Error checking information
Why Use Packets?

Advantages:

1. Efficient

Multiple users can share the network.

2. Reliable

If one packet is lost, only that packet needs retransmission.

3. Faster

Different packets can take different routes.

11. Circuit Switching vs Packet Switching
Circuit Switching

A dedicated path is created.

Example:

Traditional telephone networks.

Caller
 |
Dedicated Line
 |
Receiver
Packet Switching

Data is divided into packets.

Example:

Internet.

Packet 1 → Route A

Packet 2 → Route B

Packet 3 → Route C

The Internet uses packet switching.

12. Network Performance Factors

Performance depends on:

1. Bandwidth

Higher bandwidth = more data capacity.

2. Latency

Lower latency = faster response.

3. Packet Loss

Lost packets reduce performance.

4. Jitter

Uneven packet arrival affects real-time communication.

